#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097413} :activity/id #uuid "ef8b70d7-53d9-5368-870b-fb3c6b4e76be" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048436 :topic/id #uuid "9eb245aa-f88f-5f14-8f09-1f18ad67f8ed" :topic/math-academy-id 1076} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097413 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097817 :tutorial/content "The trigonometric expression\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}R} \\sin( x + {\\color{red}\\phi})\n$$\n\nis called the **amplitude-phase form** of sine. Here, ${\\color{blue}R}$ is the **amplitude** and ${\\color{red}\\phi}$ is the **phase** of the sine function.\n\nThe sum or difference of a sine function and a cosine function with the same argument can always be written as a sine function in amplitude-phase form. That is to say, if $a$ and $b$ are positive real numbers, then the expression\n\n$$\na \\sin x \\pm b \\cos x\n$$\n\ncan be written in the form \n\n$$\nR \\sin(x \\pm \\phi).\n$$\n\nfor some $R>0$ and $\\phi \\in \\left(0, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "69964ee4-693c-5a43-af92-88232e3d1471" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2572 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "88224b15-9a6d-5755-a5b2-af292977df30" :step/math-academy-id 11889 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097469}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097414 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097818 :tutorial/content "We've been using the amplitude-phase form for sine, but there is also an amplitude-phase form for cosine. If $a$ and $b$ are positive real numbers, then the expression\n\n$$\na \\cos x \\pm b \\sin x\n$$\n\ncan be written in the form \n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}R} \\cos(x \\mp {\\color{red}\\phi} )\n$$\n\nfor some ${\\color{blue}R}>0$ and ${\\color{red}\\phi} \\in \\left(0, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right).$ \n\nNote that the sign in the last formula flips due to the identity $\\cos(x \\mp \\phi) = \\cos x \\cos \\phi \\pm \\sin x \\sin \\phi,$ which reverses the sign when expanding.\n\nWriting this more explicitly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\operatorname{cos}x+b\\operatorname{sin}x & =R\\operatorname{cos}(x-ϕ) \\\\ a\\operatorname{cos}x-b\\operatorname{sin}x & =R\\operatorname{cos}(x+ϕ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, let's write the expression\n\n$$\n4\\sin{x}+\\cos{x}\n$$\n\nin amplitude-phase form with cosine. We start by expanding out the amplitude-phase form using the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{cos}ϕ+\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{sin}ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}ϕ\\operatorname{cos}x+R\\operatorname{sin}ϕ\\operatorname{sin}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{sin}x+\\operatorname{cos}x & =R\\operatorname{cos}(x-ϕ) \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}x+\\operatorname{cos}x & =(R\\operatorname{sin}ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}x+(R\\operatorname{cos}ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the phase $\\phi.$ Equating the coefficients of $\\sin x$ and $\\cos x,$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}ϕ=4 \\\\ R\\operatorname{cos}ϕ=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the first equation by the second one, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}ϕ} & =\\frac{4}{1} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}ϕ & =4 \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}(4) \\\\  & \\approx 1.326\\,\\text{rad}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{sin}ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{cos}ϕ)}^{2} & ={4}^{2}+{1}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({sin}^{2}ϕ+{cos}^{2}ϕ) & =16+1 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =17 \\\\ R & =\\sqrt{17}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only considered the positive root, since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{sin}x+\\operatorname{cos}x & =\\sqrt{17}\\operatorname{cos}(x-\\operatorname{arctan}(4)) \\\\  & \\approx \\sqrt{17}\\operatorname{cos}(x-1.326)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e3bb9db1-8b71-5f57-8cd5-5a113632e7d6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2574 :tutorial/title "The Amplitude-Phase Form For Cosine"} :step/id #uuid "d14d2ddb-2f8f-5f82-8559-27101b7b4ca4" :step/math-academy-id 11921 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097418}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097415 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096612 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096412 :question/id #uuid "6010c0f3-67d3-5b21-af26-695a864fb42a" :question/math-academy-id "e-3541"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74c40813-d2aa-5eff-b5dc-69a2f916f865" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097220} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097330} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244087} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244089} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244090} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244092} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292759} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302348} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302349} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302351} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302384} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335853} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348346} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348347} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348348} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348349} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373560} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374650} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374653} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374656} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374659}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Phase for the Amplitude-Phase Form for Sine"} :step/id #uuid "3819f187-e5ca-5e24-8560-2b9695d3ec9d" :step/math-academy-id 11924 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097417}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097416 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096613 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096413 :question/id #uuid "91e6008e-5c37-55aa-8cf6-cac61198f459" :question/math-academy-id "e-3542"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3dba0779-e903-5281-bc41-01b78c31a98c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049066} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049494} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049779}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096968} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096970} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244084} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244085} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244086} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302344} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302345} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302346} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302347} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348345} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373553} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374647} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375186} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375397} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375404}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Amplitude for the Amplitude-Phase Form for Sine"} :step/id #uuid "f1336a15-53ea-5387-ae8a-f35eef5dcb59" :step/math-academy-id 11925 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097415}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097417 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096614 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096414 :question/id #uuid "557b1b6e-c54a-5fa9-85e1-3cea346c3bd3" :question/math-academy-id "e-3543"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "65b4e8e7-e558-50e7-a1ec-37a6eff77e87" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096969} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097221} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244093} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244094} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244095} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244096} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247322} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247323} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292763} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302385} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302386} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302388} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302389} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348350} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348351} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348352} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348353} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348354} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373567} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373630} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374560} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374662}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Amplitude-Phase Form for Sine"} :step/id #uuid "6c1dd27c-aafa-514b-8675-0cf06d6d9413" :step/math-academy-id 11928 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097414}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097418 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096615 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096415 :question/id #uuid "9ea36397-ab9d-5567-8b38-3e83ba624e7f" :question/math-academy-id "e-3544"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f12d8028-2520-5706-ba49-d67935036af6" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048325}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096971} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097222} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244098} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292764} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292765} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292766} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297301} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297302} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302392} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302396} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302397} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335849} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335852} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335854} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373637} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373644} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374667} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374672} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374677}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Amplitude-Phase Form for Cosine"} :step/id #uuid "a827daaf-41a5-5e6d-be23-699162ae1f94" :step/math-academy-id 11929} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097469 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097831 :tutorial/content "Let's write the expression\n\n$$\n3\\sin{x}+\\sqrt{3}\\cos{x}\n$$\n\nin amplitude-phase form. We start by expanding out the amplitude-phase form using the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}(x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}ϕ+\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{sin}ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}ϕ\\operatorname{sin}x+R\\operatorname{sin}ϕ\\operatorname{cos}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}x & =R\\operatorname{sin}(x+ϕ) \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}x & =(R\\operatorname{cos}ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}x+(R\\operatorname{sin}ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the phase $\\phi$. Equating the coefficients of $\\sin x$ and $\\cos x,$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}ϕ=3 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}ϕ=\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first one, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}ϕ} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}ϕ & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{cos}ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{sin}ϕ)}^{2} & =(3)^{2}+(\\sqrt{3})^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}ϕ+{sin}^{2}ϕ) & =9+3 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =12 \\\\ R & =\\sqrt{12} \\\\ R & =2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only considered the positive root, since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n3\\sin{x}+\\sqrt{3}\\cos{x} = {\\color{blue}2\\sqrt{3}} \\sin \\left(x + {\\color{red}\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}} \\right)\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "81c76ef1-c149-53cf-a9e2-d93a243a7002" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2887 :tutorial/title "Writing a Sum or Difference of Sine and Cosine Using Amplitude-Phase Form"} :step/id #uuid "d77693ed-4059-5f7a-88e9-25300c5994e9" :step/math-academy-id 15506 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097416}}] :activity/title "Writing Sums of Trigonometric Functions in Amplitude-Phase Form" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186096873}]}
